WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.359
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:11.552
One of the things that's been really
interesting about the tech companies of

00:00:11.552 --> 00:00:15.358
the last 15 to 20 years is that,
they've not only been innovating

00:00:15.358 --> 00:00:19.441
in terms of their technology, but
also in terms of their business models.

00:00:19.441 --> 00:00:20.673
And actually,

00:00:20.673 --> 00:00:26.565
most of the successful tech companies
are some sort of platform company.

00:00:26.565 --> 00:00:32.974
Their marketplaces like Amazon matching
buyers and sellers of products or eBay.

00:00:32.974 --> 00:00:35.740
They have advertising
marketplaces like Google,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.807
all of these are platform companies.

00:00:37.807 --> 00:00:41.246
And so, not only do they have
to do technical innovation, but

00:00:41.246 --> 00:00:44.366
they also have to do business and
economic innovation.

00:00:44.366 --> 00:00:48.957
There's so many questions that come up in
operating these businesses as well as in

00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:53.225
thinking about their strategy,
they just have not been addressed before.

00:00:53.225 --> 00:00:56.551
You can't just call a consulting
company and say like,

00:00:56.551 --> 00:00:59.748
what's the best practice like I would for
a factory?

00:00:59.748 --> 00:01:04.287
If you're operating a marketplace
like in Airbnb you have buyers,

00:01:04.287 --> 00:01:09.557
consumers who want to rent and you have
sellers who are the owners of the homes.

00:01:09.557 --> 00:01:13.933
The marketplace has to really think about
both sides of the market, on the sell side

00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:17.512
of the market the homeowners have
to keep their calendars current.

00:01:17.512 --> 00:01:20.682
How annoying is it to try to
book a vacation home, and

00:01:20.682 --> 00:01:25.222
then have your reservation canceled
because in a person's oops, sorry,

00:01:25.222 --> 00:01:27.977
my calendar was wrong or wasn't available.

00:01:27.977 --> 00:01:32.422
So it's really important that the
suppliers in these marketplaces provide

00:01:32.422 --> 00:01:35.755
a high quality service,
keep their calendars current,

00:01:35.755 --> 00:01:40.357
answer questions quickly, provide good
customer service and good quality.

00:01:40.357 --> 00:01:43.810
And they only do that if they
have the incentives to do so, and

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:47.151
those incentives in turn
are created by the marketplace.

00:01:47.151 --> 00:01:51.719
So there's a real marketplace
management challenge

00:01:51.719 --> 00:01:55.278
that's new to these tech marketplaces.

00:01:55.278 --> 00:01:59.374
And then the bigger platforms have
strategic considerations that

00:01:59.374 --> 00:02:00.690
are quite profound,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:05.310
especially because a lot of these
markets tend to be pretty concentrated.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.813
When you have a platform market,
more buyers, more sellers, more sellers,

00:02:08.813 --> 00:02:11.698
more buyers, more users,
more apps, more apps, more users.

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:15.440
You don't have a lot of opportunities
to make mistakes and come back later,

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.064
Microsoft was a little bit late to
the mobile phone market even though they

00:02:19.064 --> 00:02:22.764
understood operating systems better
than Google, they missed the boat.

00:02:22.764 --> 00:02:26.346
And so they really have to
get the strategy right and

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:30.360
understand all of the issues
about industry structure.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.880
So we've seen things like, you know,
Google, they created a browser people like

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.665
Google, they're a search engine,
how can they make a browser?

00:02:36.665 --> 00:02:38.603
But if you understand
the platform economics,

00:02:38.603 --> 00:02:41.566
you understand why it was really
important for Google to have a browser.

00:02:41.566 --> 00:02:45.619
Because the browser chooses the default
search engine, and if people are using

00:02:45.619 --> 00:02:49.310
your browser then the default search
engine will be your search engine,

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:52.230
and that can be a really
important strategic advantage.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:57.529
So faced with all those very non
standard and quite challenging problems,

00:02:57.529 --> 00:03:02.237
most of the leading tech firms Have
brought in not just sort of macro

00:03:02.237 --> 00:03:08.401
economists who do forecasting which is the
traditional chief economist type of role.

00:03:08.401 --> 00:03:13.170
But they brought in micro economist who
think a lot more about these strategic

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.693
questions and
also about marketplace management and and

00:03:16.693 --> 00:03:19.200
pricing policies and things like that.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:25.352
[MUSIC]

00:03:25.352 --> 00:03:29.142
The evolution of a new tech firm is
that you have to start with an idea for

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:29.794
a product.

00:03:29.794 --> 00:03:33.694
The next part is really engineering,
you need to build the product,

00:03:33.694 --> 00:03:35.779
you need to deliver the product, and

00:03:35.779 --> 00:03:40.099
you need to incrementally make it better
until you get to some sort of scale.

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:44.776
Once you get to the point that you then
need to think about earning a profit and

00:03:44.776 --> 00:03:46.325
becoming sustainable.

00:03:46.325 --> 00:03:48.958
That's really when
the economics comes in and

00:03:48.958 --> 00:03:52.333
so a lot of times that's the stage
where a firm will start try

00:03:52.333 --> 00:03:56.603
to bring in these expertise, and
of course you hire your business people.

00:03:56.603 --> 00:04:00.434
But often these problems are so
challenging and

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:06.287
non standard that just a generic
business background may not be enough.

00:04:06.287 --> 00:04:11.540
And so, that's also a moment where
the tech economists can come in and

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:16.437
bring in sort of academic perspective
on it to be able to design new

00:04:16.437 --> 00:04:19.751
systems to design the reputation systems.

00:04:19.751 --> 00:04:21.542
To design the pricing systems,

00:04:21.542 --> 00:04:25.199
to design an auction model if
an auction model is needed and so on.

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:29.145
And they will change the companies
by teaching the engineers and

00:04:29.145 --> 00:04:34.130
teaching the machine learning folks who
might have built the basic algorithms.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:39.287
How to map the business objectives
onto their engineering,

00:04:39.287 --> 00:04:42.522
even determining what are the goals,

00:04:42.522 --> 00:04:48.506
what measures we using what KPIs
should we be using in business lingo.

00:04:48.506 --> 00:04:52.978
And it turns out that some measurements
that sound intuitive turn out not to work

00:04:52.978 --> 00:04:55.056
very well for operating a business.

00:04:55.056 --> 00:05:00.360
They might be manipulable, they might
not really represent long term success.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.986
And so getting the right measures that
really capture the organization's goals,

00:05:04.986 --> 00:05:07.563
but still can be measured
in a timely fashion,

00:05:07.563 --> 00:05:12.075
is something that requires a lot of
economics, and sort of business expertise.

00:05:12.075 --> 00:05:16.345
[MUSIC]

00:05:16.345 --> 00:05:21.315
The tech firms really need economists who
can think about strategic questions like

00:05:21.315 --> 00:05:25.788
industry structure that requires
understanding of sort of game theory and

00:05:25.788 --> 00:05:27.495
industrial organization.

00:05:27.495 --> 00:05:30.198
Turns out that some industry
structures make sense and

00:05:30.198 --> 00:05:31.910
some are really not sustainable.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:35.439
And then there's more of
the microeconomic marketplace management.

00:05:35.439 --> 00:05:39.500
If you have a marketplace like a Airbnb or
like a Yelp,

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:45.095
which is a review platform, you need
to really think about the incentives

00:05:45.095 --> 00:05:50.259
of all the people involved and
you need to manage those incentives.

00:05:50.259 --> 00:05:53.899
So there's the economic theory
around what those incentives are and

00:05:53.899 --> 00:05:57.108
designing incentives carefully for
all the participants.

00:05:57.108 --> 00:06:00.924
Then there's also the measurement, we need
to know how strong those incentives have

00:06:00.924 --> 00:06:03.257
to be, we need to measure
what the big problems are.

00:06:03.257 --> 00:06:09.232
And we design experiments that test
the the impact of innovations.

00:06:09.232 --> 00:06:12.959
And actually designing experiments in
a marketplace is really quite difficult

00:06:12.959 --> 00:06:14.913
because there's equilibrium effects.

00:06:14.913 --> 00:06:18.569
If I can't just change things for
1% of the suppliers and

00:06:18.569 --> 00:06:22.980
expect that to have the same effect
as if I change it for all suppliers.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:27.544
It's different if you and all of
your competitors experience a change

00:06:27.544 --> 00:06:32.338
versus if just a single supplier
experiences that change by themselves,

00:06:32.338 --> 00:06:36.153
their concerns about antitrust and
competition policy.

00:06:36.153 --> 00:06:40.040
There are concerns about privacy and
security,

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:44.033
there are concerns about
new service providers.

00:06:44.033 --> 00:06:48.406
How do we think about regulating
the Ubers and the Lifts and so

00:06:48.406 --> 00:06:53.055
on, what are the rules that apply
to these new business models?

00:06:53.055 --> 00:06:57.007
And so, for
all of those types of questions,

00:06:57.007 --> 00:07:02.416
the economist role is to both
conceptualize what the costs and

00:07:02.416 --> 00:07:08.359
benefits of what's going on, and
also again to bring data to bear.

00:07:08.359 --> 00:07:10.501
[MUSIC]

00:07:10.501 --> 00:07:13.066
The relationship between academia and

00:07:13.066 --> 00:07:16.956
business has really changed
with the advent of tech firms.

00:07:16.956 --> 00:07:21.322
Historically, there was a bit of a divide,
you were an academic, or

00:07:21.322 --> 00:07:22.993
you were part of the firm.

00:07:22.993 --> 00:07:27.777
But because there's so many new problems
